        An optical frequency comb is a laser source whose spectrum consists of a
        large number of discrete, equally spaced lines spanning a broad optical
        bandwidth. Generated by a mode-locked femtosecond laser, the comb
        provides a direct, phase-coherent link between radio-frequency standards
        and optical frequencies. This document explains the physical origin of the
        comb, the two degrees of freedom that must be stabilised ($f_"ceo"$ and
        $f_"rep"$), the $f$-to-$2f$ self-referencing technique used to detect the
        carrier-envelope offset, and the procedure for phase-locking an external
        continuous-wave laser to a comb tooth via beatnote detection. The
        conditions under which a beatnote lock fails — including the pull-out
        condition for a PLL tracking a rapidly shifting beatnote — are discussed,
        along with digital and hardware strategies for improving lock robustness.
      ]
    ]
  ]
]

