= Physical Origin of the Frequency Comb

== Mode-Locked Lasers

A mode-locked laser produces a periodic train of ultrashort optical pulses by
forcing many longitudinal cavity modes to oscillate simultaneously with a fixed
phase relationship. The round-trip time of the cavity is $T_"rep" = 2L / v_g$,
where $L$ is the cavity length and $v_g$ is the group velocity of the pulse
envelope. Each pulse is separated from the next by exactly $T_"rep"$, giving a
pulse repetition frequency

$
f_"rep" = 1 / T_"rep"
$

In practice, $f_"rep"$ lies in the range 100 MHz to 10 GHz depending on the
cavity length. The pulse duration is typically 10–100 fs, making these lasers
extremely broadband: a 30 fs pulse centred at 800 nm has a transform-limited
spectral width of roughly 30 THz.

== From Pulse Train to Frequency Comb

The Fourier transform of a perfectly periodic train of identical pulses is a
*comb* of Dirac delta functions, each separated by $f_"rep"$. Real mode-locked
lasers introduce one additional complication: the group velocity and phase
velocity inside the cavity are not equal due to dispersion. As a result, the
carrier wave of each pulse is slightly phase-shifted relative to the envelope of
the previous pulse by an amount $Delta phi_"ceo"$ per round trip. This
carrier-envelope phase slip accumulates from pulse to pulse and shifts the entire
comb spectrum away from zero by the *carrier-envelope offset frequency*:

$
f_"ceo" = frac(Delta phi_"ceo", 2 pi) dot f_"rep", quad f_"ceo" in [0, f_"rep")
$

The frequency of the $n$-th comb tooth is therefore

$
#rect(inset: 8pt)[
  $f_n = f_"ceo" + n dot f_"rep"$
]
$

where $n$ is a large positive integer — typically $3 times 10^5$ to $10^6$ for
teeth in the visible or near-infrared. Because $f_"rep"$ and $f_"ceo"$ are both
radio frequencies (below 1 GHz), they can be measured directly with fast
electronics and stabilised to an absolute reference with sub-hertz precision.
Every comb tooth is then phase-coherently anchored to that reference, realising
an *optical clockwork* that connects the microwave and optical domains.

