*Feedforward* deserves special mention for digital implementations. If the
error signals from the $f_"rep"$ and $f_"ceo"$ stabilisation loops are available
as digital monitor outputs, they encode the instantaneous fluctuations of every
comb tooth. These can be combined and applied as a feedforward correction to the
CW laser actuator, reducing the residual beatnote motion seen by the servo loop.
This relaxes the bandwidth requirement and can be implemented entirely in
software if the comb electronics provide suitable outputs.

= Summary

The frequency comb is characterised by two radio-frequency parameters —
$f_"rep"$ and $f_"ceo"$ — that together specify the position of every tooth in
the optical spectrum via $f_n = f_"ceo" + n dot f_"rep"$. Stabilising both
parameters to a microwave reference transfers its accuracy to every tooth.
A CW laser is locked to the comb by phase-locking the beatnote
$f_"beat" = |f_"laser" - f_n|$ to a reference frequency, yielding an absolutely
known optical frequency $f_"laser" = f_"ceo" + n dot f_"rep" + f_"ref"$.
The primary failure mode is a rapidly shifting beatnote exceeding the PLL
pull-out limit $dot(f)_"beat" = 2 pi B^2$; the remedy is higher servo bandwidth,
faster actuators, or feedforward cancellation of the comb fluctuations.
